WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.320
Any technique that we can come up with
that makes it interesting for us is

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.460
actually beneficial for our language
learning because it keeps us engaged.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.330
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here again,
and today I wanna talk about a new

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:18.540
approach to working with your language
content when you are in the plateau.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.310
Now, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:23.640
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.900
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.800
I do appreciate it.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.460
Now many of you may remember when
I talked about the hockey stick.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:36.420
Okay.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:39.930
And the story there is that
when we start in the language,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.310
we have a very steep climb.

00:00:42.089 --> 00:00:45.839
But it's kind of satisfying because
we're seeing, oh, maybe this way you

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:50.910
can see the different colors, we're
encountering high frequency words quite

00:00:50.915 --> 00:00:54.750
often and so it's easier to learn them
because they they're high frequency.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.370
They show up more often, but once we sort
of turn the corner here, and we're on

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.810
this long road, this shaft of the hockey
stick, we're going from essentially

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.150
A2 or B1 and we wanna reach B2.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.320
B2 is that comfortable spot
where you can understand

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.150
movies, you still make mistakes.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.300
There's still gaps in what you
under in your comprehension, but

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.240
you're, you're kind of launched
you're on your own, but here...

00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:21.524
right now in my Persian,
I'm be in between that.

00:01:21.585 --> 00:01:22.125
Okay.

00:01:22.395 --> 00:01:25.274
So I've, I've developed a new
technique that I wanna share with you.

00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:28.065
It might be helpful for you
in your language learning.

00:01:28.304 --> 00:01:35.595
So what I do is I go to my normal source
of podcasts in Farsi, which is  Radio

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:41.265
Farad, which is, you know, Radio Free
Europe, Persian language edition, and

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:46.425
every day there are a number of podcasts
talking about the news in Iran, the news

00:01:46.425 --> 00:01:48.525
in the Middle East, the news in the world.

00:01:49.065 --> 00:01:53.085
And it has a lot of good vocabulary
for things that I'm interested in.

00:01:53.775 --> 00:01:55.215
So I download this podcast.

00:01:55.935 --> 00:01:58.545
I get a transcript on Happy Scribe.

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:03.365
I importthe MP3 file and
the transcripts into LingQ.

00:02:03.524 --> 00:02:07.365
So now I have a lesson it's
30 minutes of audio and the

00:02:07.365 --> 00:02:09.375
corresponding amount of text.

00:02:09.794 --> 00:02:13.725
Now because it's written in the Arabic
script it actually takes me a long time

00:02:13.725 --> 00:02:16.125
to read, takes me 30 minutes to listen.

00:02:16.334 --> 00:02:18.785
It takes me maybe an hour and a
half to read through this thing.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.040
I'm at the point now where
I know most of the words.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:27.600
So it's of course, good to read
again, develop better fluency at

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.730
reading, but it also slows me down.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.380
So what I find myself more and more
doing, I may start reading the whole

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.750
thing and then perhaps, because I'm
impatient by nature I start jumping.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.829
So I jump from yellow word to yellow word.

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:47.850
In other words, the yellow words
are words that I have previously met

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:52.980
that I have looked up that I have a
meaning for, but they're not yet known.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.359
In fact, some of them, they, that I
might just have met are almost like new

00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:01.275
words to me except they're no longer
blue because I've met them before.

00:03:01.335 --> 00:03:03.615
So I look them up again
and again and again.

00:03:04.035 --> 00:03:08.835
So what I tend to do now is I skip
from yellow word to yellow word.

00:03:08.924 --> 00:03:13.035
There's the occasional blue word so I
look that word up, it becomes yellow.

00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:17.084
And I go through my lesson that way
and I can go through a 30 minute,

00:03:17.535 --> 00:03:20.234
you know, audio file quite quickly.

00:03:20.445 --> 00:03:23.775
And I'm focusing in on the
words that I don't yet know.

00:03:24.705 --> 00:03:29.055
To a large extent are the words that I
didn't understand when I was listening.

00:03:29.205 --> 00:03:33.704
Now, there will be words that
I have made known, and I hear

00:03:33.704 --> 00:03:35.025
them and I don't recognize them.

00:03:35.025 --> 00:03:37.125
So nothing is perfect
in language learning.

00:03:37.305 --> 00:03:43.095
However, it is enabling me to focus in
on words that are on my sort of saved

00:03:43.095 --> 00:03:45.975
LingQs list, but are not yet known.

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:50.445
And I have like, I don't know,
27,000 saved links in Persian.

00:03:50.625 --> 00:03:52.994
I have only 10,000 known words.

00:03:53.174 --> 00:03:57.015
I wanna move that known word
number up to 15 or 20,000.

00:03:57.225 --> 00:04:01.635
If I'm at that level, I think I will
be able to understand a lot more

00:04:01.875 --> 00:04:04.185
and eventually to speak more easily.

00:04:04.335 --> 00:04:09.465
So I just jump from the yellow word to the
next yellow word, to the next yellow word.

00:04:09.735 --> 00:04:11.985
And I'm finding that quite effective.

00:04:12.495 --> 00:04:19.065
One advantage is if I'm in a, a lesson
on economics, then certain economic

00:04:19.065 --> 00:04:21.075
terms will show up fairly often.

00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:26.820
So if I'm jumping from term to term
to term, In a lesson on say economics

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:31.560
or, uh, teachers strike or whatever
might be happening I tend to come

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:37.190
up with the same word that teachers
union or hunger strike or whatever.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.230
So I'm actually encountering
these words fairly often.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:45.270
I'm also then able to go and listen
again and see if I don't understand

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.860
more and which I typically do.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:51.180
So I, I get a sense of satisfaction
that I've gone through this lesson.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.070
Uh, and what's more uh, up on, you
know, until I started doing this, I

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:02.200
found it difficult to keep up with my
listening because, uh, at Radio Farda

00:05:02.219 --> 00:05:08.340
they have their, you know, uh, ..., they
have their ..., they have their midday

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.380
podcast, they have a number of these.

00:05:10.409 --> 00:05:14.159
And I wanna process more of the language,
listen to the more of the language

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:18.300
quickly, go through it, to pick out
the words that I didn't understand,

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.550
perhaps move some of them to known.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:26.414
And this, the technique that I'm
using, I would call it sort of speed

00:05:26.414 --> 00:05:29.174
learning when you're in the plateau.

00:05:29.955 --> 00:05:35.865
So it's also more enjoyable because
it sometimes can be tiring to continue

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.604
to read essentially the same words
that you know, and then struggle with

00:05:40.604 --> 00:05:42.075
the words that you don't yet know.

00:05:42.164 --> 00:05:43.905
Whereas this way I'm able to focus in.

00:05:44.145 --> 00:05:48.539
The interesting thing is if I try
to deal with these yellow words

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.344
in a list which I sometimes do.

00:05:51.344 --> 00:05:56.655
I might look at them as a list, say
for that lesson before I go skipping.

00:05:57.375 --> 00:06:01.784
Uh, but if I look at them in a sort of
list in the vocabulary section, which

00:06:01.784 --> 00:06:07.724
I also sometimes do, uh, it's not,
it's more devoid of context, at least

00:06:07.724 --> 00:06:09.155
when I'm skipping through the lesson.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.430
I can always look for additional context
to the left or to the right of the

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.320
word, that yellow word that I landed on.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.740
So it's a little more meaningful
than just going through a list.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:24.750
So I don't know if that's helpful to
helpful to you, but I just thought I

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.690
would share with you my latest technique.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.400
And I admit that I sometimes move from one
new technique to another new technique.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:37.349
I tend to blow a bit hot and cold, which
isn't a bad thing because I think in our

00:06:37.349 --> 00:06:38.940
language learning variety is important.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:43.320
Variety is important to keep it
interesting and anything, any

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.280
technique that we can come up with
that makes it interesting for us is

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.510
actually beneficial for our language
learning because it keeps us engaged.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.610
So on that subject, I wanna leave a
couple of videos, one about listening

00:06:56.615 --> 00:07:01.150
and one about reading, but I do
consider both forms of consuming

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.240
input to be very much linked.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.140
And there may be other techniques that
you out there can come up with that

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.565
enable us to combine our listening
activities with our reading activities.

00:07:11.745 --> 00:07:12.705
Thank you for listening.

00:07:12.885 --> 00:07:13.575
Bye for now.

